n terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom the missing expression are $\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T).$" "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom the missing expression are $\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186272285 :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" "$T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?"] [:db/cas 17592186272285 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expressions are ${R}^{n}$ and $n.$" "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expressions are ${R}^{n}$ and $n.$"] [:db/cas 17592186272287 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$T$ is not invertible” is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$T$ is not invertible”; $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$T$ is not invertible” is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$T$ is not invertible”; $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186272288 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement “$T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$” is false. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0},$ which means there is no non-zero vector $v$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$; $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement “$T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$” is false. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0},$ which means there is no non-zero vector $v$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$; $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186272291 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$ is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$ is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$ is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$ is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186272301 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4}$ is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is not invertible” is true. Indeed, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$ is true. Indeed, since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$T$ is not invertible”; $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4}$ is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is not invertible” is true. Indeed, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$ is true. Indeed, since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$T$ is not invertible”; $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186272302 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$ is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible. Since $T$ is not invertible, by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is not a bijection” is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$; “$T$ is not a bijection” only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$ is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible. Since $T$ is not invertible, by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is not a bijection” is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$; “$T$ is not a bijection” only."] [:db/cas 17592186272319 :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{6\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ units up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $D-B=1-\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)+1.\n$$" "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{6\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ units up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle D-B=1-\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)+1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272341 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272342 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272347 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$ is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)<4$ is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(A)<4$ only." "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$ is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)<4$ is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(A)<4$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186272349 :question/worked-solution "If $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{8} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times$  \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}.$*  $\\,\\times$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $8$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=8,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $8$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has $8$ pivot columns” only." "If $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{8} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times$  \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}.$*  $\\,\\times$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $8$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=8,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $8$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has $8$ pivot columns” only."] [:db/cas 17592186272351 :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}.$" "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272368 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186272369 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275105 :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}$  that satisfies $y(1)=2.$" "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\n$$  that satisfies $y(1)=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186275125 :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{12}{5}$ units down. Hence, $D=-\\frac{12}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{5}+\\left(-\\frac{12}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{9}{5}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)-\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$" "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{12}{5}$ units down. Hence, $D=-\\frac{12}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B+D=\\frac{3}{5}+\\left(-\\frac{12}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{9}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)-\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275126 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+3C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/29/29346a073d6a22f0bab74eeac86431e8c761c9ed21a005ec6965f71e6436c117.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)$ by $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n3C=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+3C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/29/29346a073d6a22f0bab74eeac86431e8c761c9ed21a005ec6965f71e6436c117.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n3C=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186275127 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/59/595222a0a6987c1fae1a7b245d2936eba08312357015cc6f78c66eb60f942707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/59/595222a0a6987c1fae1a7b245d2936eba08312357015cc6f78c66eb60f942707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5\\pi }{12}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186275129 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/e1/e1ed3a322c82b5c70acb01c54c97b09814f41aec80d980034a6fcdc46037209c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/e1/e1ed3a322c82b5c70acb01c54c97b09814f41aec80d980034a6fcdc46037209c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275130 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{4}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/4c/4c5147d95cc4d5df1d847f5fe9f580d9595d0f4d8c2c8209efa1f2a3cd9c5aea.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)$ by $\\frac{9\\pi }{40}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\n$$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{4}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/4c/4c5147d95cc4d5df1d847f5fe9f580d9595d0f4d8c2c8209efa1f2a3cd9c5aea.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{9\\pi }{40}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275131 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{5}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/1b/1b3e3365d8047befbe70c2371a44e61262c68a2da694ab410ae974917e8535bd.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{5}\\right)$ by $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))\n$$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{5}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/1b/1b3e3365d8047befbe70c2371a44e61262c68a2da694ab410ae974917e8535bd.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{5}\\right)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186275132 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{3C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/01/015ca9336646a7545de842414ace2c2a695688e30653b2ed019000d69609d56d.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)$ by $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{3C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))\n$$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/01/015ca9336646a7545de842414ace2c2a695688e30653b2ed019000d69609d56d.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186275141 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.4+0.3+0.2+0.1=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” and ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$, it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”; ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$; “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function”." "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1\n$$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.4+0.3+0.2+0.1=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1,\n$$ it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1;\n$$ “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function”."] [:db/cas 17592186275142 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.42+0.18+0.33+0.07=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” and ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$, it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”; ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$; “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function”." "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1\n$$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.42+0.18+0.33+0.07=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1,\n$$ it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1;\n$$ “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function”."] [:db/cas 17592186275143 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ is false. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.5+0.1+0.1+0.2=0.9\\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is false. Because $f(x,y)$ does not satisfy condition ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$, it is *not* a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” only." "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1\n$$ is false. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.5+0.1+0.1+0.2=0.9\\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is false. Because $f(x,y)$ does not satisfy condition $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1,\n$$ it is *not* a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186275144 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is false. The values of $f(2,1)$ and $f(3,3)$ in the table does not satisfy $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =-0.1+0.2+0.2+0.4+0.6-0.3=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is false. Because $f(x,y)$ does not satisfy condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”, it is *not* a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ only." "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is false. The values of $f(2,1)$ and $f(3,3)$ in the table does not satisfy $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1\n$$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =-0.1+0.2+0.2+0.4+0.6-0.3=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is false. Because $f(x,y)$ does not satisfy condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”, it is *not* a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186275145 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is false. The value of $f(2,2)$ in the table does not satisfy $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.2+0.15+0.16-0.1+0.24+0.35=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is false. Because $f(x,y)$ does not satisfy condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”, it is *not* a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ only." "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is false. The value of $f(2,2)$ in the table does not satisfy $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1\n$$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.2+0.15+0.16-0.1+0.24+0.35=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is false. Because $f(x,y)$ does not satisfy condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”, it is *not* a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186275151 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275151 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+5\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+5\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275152 :question/problem "$\\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275152 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+C \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+C \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275154 :question/problem "![](images/c6/c677b6f589e1b4d76e31ff187c0ea09d756f2a24130d3c8b1dda220400fb9910.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/c6/c677b6f589e1b4d76e31ff187c0ea09d756f2a24130d3c8b1dda220400fb9910.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275173 :question/problem "$\\int 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275174 :question/problem "Solve for $p$ where $\\frac{1}{p}=\\frac{10}{9p}+\\frac{p-1}{9p}.$" "Solve for $p$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{p}=\\frac{10}{9p}+\\frac{p-1}{9p}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275186 :question/problem "![](images/64/64f3e5d99f302a567d6299ee5036a1efa728bae0e21f5905d3698241e1abdfa1.png)\n\nConsider the gra